(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-physiology-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"150 mM NaCl and 300 mM glucose both equal approximately what osmolarity, and why?","choices":["0 mOsm/L for both, since neither solute is considered capable of dissociating in aqueous solution","They cannot be meaningfully compared, since molarity and osmolarity measure entirely unrelated solution properties","300 mOsm/L, because NaCl dissociates into two particles (Na+ and Cl-) per molecule while glucose does not dissociate","150 mOsm/L, because glucose does not dissociate while NaCl also remains fully associated in solution"],"answer":2,"exp":"150 mM NaCl dissociates into 2 particles per molecule (Na+ and Cl-), yielding 300 mOsm/L, matching 300 mM glucose (which does not dissociate) at 300 mOsm/L — both represent 300 mOsm/L. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which of the following is classified as a permeant molecule (crosses the membrane almost as fast as water)?","choices":["Free amino acids","Charged sodium ions","Urea and glycerol","Glucose molecules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Permeant molecules like urea and glycerol have relatively high lipid solubility and move through the cell membrane almost as fast as water, unlike impermeant molecules such as ions, glucose, and amino acids. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What determines steady-state cell volume?","choices":["The concentration of permeant particles like urea, dissolved within the extracellular fluid","The total number of mitochondria present within the cytoplasm of a given cell","The concentration of impermeant particles in the extracellular fluid (e.g., Na+, K+, proteins)","The rate of DNA replication occurring during the S phase of the cell division cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Steady-state cell volume depends on the concentration of impermeant particles in the extracellular fluid (Na+, K+, proteins); permeant particles like urea and glycerol cause only transient changes in cell volume. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the key difference between tonicity and osmolarity?","choices":["Osmolarity depends on the membrane's permeability characteristics; tonicity does not depend on the membrane at all","Tonicity only applies to dissolved gases within the extracellular fluid compartment","Osmolarity is simply a measure of solute concentration; tonicity (effective osmotic pressure) depends on both the membrane and the solute's permeability","They are identical concepts that both depend solely on the total solute particle concentration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Osmolarity is merely another way of expressing the concentration of a solution, while tonicity (effective osmotic pressure) depends on the properties of both the membrane and the solute — specifically its permeability. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Roughly what fraction of a typical cell's energy — and up to what fraction of a neuron's energy — does the Na+-K+ ATPase require?","choices":["About 1/100 of any cell's energy, up to 1/10 of a neuron's energy","None — the pump runs passively with no energy cost","About 1/5 of a typical cell's energy, up to 2/3 of a neuron's energy","Nearly all of a cell's energy budget, up to 9/10 of a neuron's energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase requires about 1/5 of a typical cell's energy budget and up to 2/3 of a neuron's energy, reflecting how metabolically demanding maintaining ionic gradients is, especially in excitable cells. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Is 300 mOsm/L urea isotonic to a typical cell (300 mOsm/L)?","choices":["No, because 300 mOsm/L urea is not actually isosmotic to the cell's own 300 mOsm/L interior","Yes, because it is isosmotic, and any isosmotic solution is by definition also isotonic","No — although it is isosmotic, urea is a permeant solute that crosses into the cell, so it is not isotonic","Yes, because urea never crosses cell membranes, behaving as a fully impermeant solute like sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"300 mOsm/L urea is isosmotic (same osmolarity as the cell) but not isotonic — because urea is a permeant solute, it crosses into the cell rather than exerting sustained osmotic pressure, so the cell will swell and burst. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A cell (300 mOsm/L) is placed in a solution of 300 mOsm/L glycerol plus 200 mOsm/L NaCl. What happens to cell volume?","choices":["The cell shrinks and stays shrunken","No net change occurs at all","The cell swells and stays swollen","The cell shrinks then swells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because the impermeant NaCl (200 mOsm/L) is initially lower than the cell's 300 mOsm/L, the cell first shrinks; but the permeant glycerol equilibrates into the cell over time, causing it to subsequently swell. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A cell (300 mOsm/L) is placed in 200 mOsm/L urea. What happens to cell volume?","choices":["No change occurs","Shrinks and stays shrunk","Shrinks then swells","Swells and bursts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because urea is a permeant solute, the cell in 200 mOsm/L urea swells and bursts as urea and water move into the cell. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What ionic condition is required for motile cilia to produce their whiplike movements (in addition to ATP)?","choices":["Complete absence of sodium and potassium ions","Appropriate ionic conditions, e.g., Ca2+","High extracellular osmolarity only","High extracellular glucose concentration alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motile cilia movement requires ATP and appropriate ionic conditions, such as Ca2+, similar to the flagellum of sperm. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Approximately how many times does water in a red blood cell exchange during its transport through a capillary?","choices":["100 times","10 times","1000 times","1 time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Water in a red blood cell exchanges approximately 100 times during its transport through a capillary, reflecting the astounding rapidity of water diffusion through aquaporins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In transcellular transport of glucose and amino acids across an epithelial cell, what drives the initial entry of glucose/AA from the lumen into the cell?","choices":["Osmotic pressure alone, independent of any sodium ion gradient","Secondary active transport coupled to the Na+ gradient (Na+ co-transport)","Direct ATP hydrolysis by the glucose transporter itself","Simple diffusion alone with no ion involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucose and amino acid entry from the intestinal/renal lumen into epithelial cells is driven by secondary active transport, coupled to the Na+ electrochemical gradient (Na+/glucose and Na+/AA symporters). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What does the existence of molecular concentration gradients across the cell membrane primarily reflect?","choices":["The complete absence of any membrane transport proteins, allowing free unrestricted diffusion of every molecule","Purely random thermodynamic chance, with no biological significance or underlying regulatory mechanism","Equal concentrations of every molecule maintained identically inside and outside the cell membrane","The selective permeability and active transport mechanisms of the membrane, maintaining different intracellular vs extracellular ion/molecule concentrations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Molecular gradients across the cell membrane (e.g., different concentrations of Na+, K+, and other solutes inside vs. outside the cell) reflect the membrane's selective permeability and active transport mechanisms. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"According to the size principle of motor unit recruitment, which motor units are recruited first?","choices":["The smallest, highly excitable motor units containing Type 1 fibers; larger Type 2 units are recruited only when greater force is needed","Motor units are recruited randomly regardless of size, with the CNS selecting fibers based only on which are anatomically closest to the descending axon","The largest, least excitable motor units containing Type 2 fibers are recruited first, with smaller Type 1 units activated only as force demand increases","Recruitment order has no relationship to unit size, being instead determined solely by the fiber's distance from the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"The size principle states that smaller motor units (containing Type 1 fibers) are recruited before larger ones — weak CNS signals activate the highly excitable small units first, with larger Type 2 units recruited only when greater force is needed. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What are the two mechanisms of force summation in skeletal muscle?","choices":["Only frequency summation exists; multiple fiber recruitment produces no meaningful additional increase in overall muscle force output","Summation occurs only at the neuromuscular junction as accumulating end-plate potentials, with no corresponding mechanism operating within the muscle fibers themselves","Multiple fiber summation (recruiting more motor units) and frequency summation (increasing stimulation frequency of a single motor unit)","Temporal summation and spatial summation of action potentials at the presynaptic terminal, without any contribution from motor unit recruitment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Force summation results from multiple fiber summation (an increase in the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, i.e., recruitment) and frequency summation (an increase in the stimulation frequency of a single motor unit). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is a \"fused tetanus\" contraction?","choices":["A sustained contraction that occurs only in smooth muscle, arising because smooth muscle fibers lack any refractory period between successive action potentials","A progressive form of muscle atrophy caused by chronic understimulation of the motor unit, occurring gradually over weeks to months of disuse","A contraction where individual twitches are no longer resolved because action potential frequency is sufficiently high that the muscle is restimulated before complete relaxation","A single isolated twitch produced by one action potential, followed by complete relaxation before the next stimulus arrives"],"answer":2,"exp":"If action potential frequency is high enough that a muscle is restimulated before complete relaxation from the previous twitch, the twitches summate and individual contractions are no longer resolved — a \"fused tetanus.\" — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the difference between muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia as growth responses?","choices":["Hypertrophy is the rare formation of entirely new muscle fibers, typically triggered by endurance training over months; hyperplasia is the common increase in actin/myosin content within existing fibers seen with weight lifting","Hypertrophy (common) is increased production of actin/myosin and splitting of myofibrils within existing fibers; hyperplasia (rare) is the formation of new muscle fibers, sometimes from endurance training","They are the same underlying process, differing only in the medical terminology used depending on whether the change is described in skeletal versus cardiac or smooth muscle","Neither process increases force generation; both instead improve fatigue resistance by increasing mitochondrial density within existing fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypertrophy (common, occurs over weeks, e.g., from weight lifting) involves increased production of actin and myosin with myofibril splitting within existing fibers; hyperplasia (rare) is the formation of new muscle fibers, which can be caused by endurance exercise training — both increase force generation without changing shortening capacity or max velocity. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What can cause muscle fasciculations (involuntary, visible muscle twitches)?","choices":["High plasma calcium only, since hypercalcemia is thought to raise the resting membrane potential of motor neurons closer to threshold, triggering spontaneous repetitive firing and visible twitching","Low plasma magnesium (which blocks ACh release) or low plasma calcium (which lowers the threshold potential of voltage-gated sodium channels), among other causes","Only motor neuron disease can cause fasciculations, since intact motor neurons are electrically stable and cannot depolarize spontaneously under any other circumstance","Fasciculations have no known physiological cause and are considered a purely psychogenic phenomenon unrelated to any measurable change in nerve or muscle membrane properties"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle fasciculations result from spontaneous depolarization of a motor neuron, and can be caused by motor neuron disease, hypomagnesemia (Mg2+ blocks ACh release, so low Mg2+ promotes unwanted transmission), hypocalcemia (lowers the threshold potential, causing unplanned APs), over-exertion, or excessive alcohol. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What distinguishes unitary (visceral) smooth muscle from multiunit smooth muscle?","choices":["Both types are identical in innervation pattern, each composed of electrically coupled cells forming a syncytium that contracts spontaneously via pacemaker activity, with autonomic input serving only to modulate rather than initiate contraction","Multiunit smooth muscle is found only in the gastrointestinal tract, where it forms electrically coupled sheets of spontaneously active cells that propagate contraction via gap junctions without requiring direct innervation of each cell","Unitary smooth muscle is made of discrete, densely innervated bundles of cells that contract only in direct response to their individual innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris); multiunit smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that often acts spontaneously as a syncytium (e.g., gut, blood vessels)","Unitary smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that often acts spontaneously (a syncytium, e.g., gut, blood vessels); multiunit smooth muscle is made of discrete, densely innervated bundles that contract only in response to innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Unitary (visceral) smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells acting in unison, often spontaneously active (a syncytium, e.g., gut and blood vessels), while multiunit smooth muscle is made of discrete bundles of densely innervated cells that contract only in response to their innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris, piloerectors). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is the \"latch state\" of smooth muscle?","choices":["A brief, high-energy contraction phase lasting only milliseconds, consuming roughly 100 times more oxygen than an equivalent skeletal muscle contraction","A pathological state in which the muscle cannot contract at all, caused by permanent dephosphorylation of the myosin regulatory light chain by phosphatase","The ability to maintain force for long periods (hours to weeks) very efficiently","A rapid twitch response unique to smooth muscle, lasting only a few milliseconds before complete relaxation occurs and consuming very high amounts of ATP"],"answer":2,"exp":"Smooth muscle can maintain force for long periods (hours, days, or weeks) via the \"latch state,\" reflecting its very high energy efficiency (oxygen consumption is only about 1% of an equally-tensioned skeletal muscle). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What protein does Ca2+ bind to in smooth muscle contraction, since troponin is absent?","choices":["Tropomyosin","Titin","Calsequestrin","Calmodulin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Smooth muscle lacks the troponin complex; instead, Ca2+ binds to calmodulin, which is structurally similar to troponin C and also binds Ca2+, activating myosin light chain kinase (MLCK). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Is smooth muscle contraction regulated at the level of actin or myosin, and what enzyme initiates it?","choices":["Neither actin-based nor myosin-based regulation exists in smooth muscle; contraction instead occurs continuously whenever any calcium at all is present in the cytosol, with no distinct enzymatic regulatory step required","Actin-based regulation, via troponin: the Ca2+-calmodulin complex binds directly to troponin C on the thin filament, exposing the myosin-binding site in essentially the same way as skeletal and cardiac muscle","Regulation occurs entirely at the level of the SR calcium pump (SERCA), which continuously cycles calcium in and out of the cytosol to directly time each individual cross-bridge cycle","Myosin-based regulation: myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), activated by Ca2+-calmodulin, phosphorylates the myosin regulatory light chain to enable ATP hydrolysis and cross-bridge cycling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Unlike the actin-based (troponin) regulation of skeletal/cardiac muscle, smooth muscle contraction is myosin-based: the Ca2+-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), which phosphorylates the myosin regulatory light chain, allowing the myosin head to hydrolyze ATP and bind actin. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Unlike skeletal muscle, what is an important source of Ca2+ for smooth muscle contraction?","choices":["Only the sarcoplasmic reticulum, exactly as in skeletal muscle, since smooth muscle plasma membrane lacks the voltage-gated calcium channels found in cardiac tissue","Smooth muscle requires no calcium at all for contraction, relying instead entirely on myosin light chain phosphorylation triggered by passive mechanical stretch of the fiber","Ca2+ entering through channels in the plasma membrane (Ca2+ action potentials), similar to cardiac muscle","Only calcium bound to titin, which is thought to release it into the cytosol whenever the filament is passively stretched beyond its normal resting length"],"answer":2,"exp":"Smooth muscle can generate Ca2+ action potentials, with Ca2+ entering through membrane channels serving as an important calcium source — similar to cardiac muscle and unlike skeletal muscle, which relies solely on SR calcium release. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does control of smooth muscle by the autonomic nervous system (e.g., norepinephrine, acetylcholine) differ from control at the skeletal neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Norepinephrine has no direct effect on smooth muscle receptors at all, acting only on cardiac and skeletal muscle tissue through entirely separate adrenergic signaling pathways","The same neurotransmitter can sometimes relax and sometimes contract smooth muscle depending on the tissue/receptor, unlike ACh at the skeletal NMJ which is always excitatory","Smooth muscle receives no autonomic innervation whatsoever, generating and propagating all of its contractile activity purely through intrinsic myogenic pacemaker cells","It is identical in mechanism to the skeletal NMJ and always excitatory, since acetylcholine binds the same nicotinic receptor type in both smooth and skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unlike the skeletal NMJ (where ACh is always excitatory), autonomic neurotransmitters like norepinephrine and acetylcholine can sometimes relax and sometimes contract smooth muscle, depending on the specific tissue and receptor subtype involved. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What causes normal resting muscle tone?","choices":["Complete absence of nerve signals to the muscle, which instead maintains partial contraction through an intrinsic myogenic mechanism independent of innervation","Ongoing subconscious nerve impulses (and signals originating in muscle spindles) that keep groups of muscle fibers in a constant state of partial, involuntary contraction","Voluntary continuous contraction only, requiring conscious cortical drive that ceases entirely and produces complete flaccidity whenever attention is diverted or during sleep","Muscle tone has no neural basis at all, arising instead purely from the passive elastic properties of connective tissue and titin within the sarcomere"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle tone results from ongoing subconscious nerve impulses (and signals from muscle spindles) that keep muscle fibers in a constant state of partial, involuntary contraction; loss of nerve supply causes flaccidity and eventual muscle wasting. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"At approximately what temperature do cold receptors show their peak response, and at what temperature do warm receptors peak?","choices":["Cold receptors peak at 25°C (responding 7-44°C); warm receptors peak at 44°C (responding 30-49°C)","Cold receptors peak at 15°C (responding 5-40°C); warm receptors peak at 40°C (responding 25-45°C)","Cold receptors peak at 10°C, while warm receptors respond maximally only above 50°C, near the pain threshold","Both peak at the same temperature range (30-35°C), overlapping almost completely near normal skin temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cold receptors respond across 7-44°C with peak response at 25°C, while warm receptors respond across 30-49°C with peak response at 44°C; the relative degree of stimulation of each receptor type determines the perceived temperature sensation. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Where is cold receptor density highest and lowest on the body?","choices":["Highest on the palms only","Highest on the lips, lowest on the trunk","Highest on the trunk, lowest on the lips","Equal everywhere on the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cold receptor density is highest on the lips and lowest on the trunk; there are also many more cold receptors than warm receptors overall. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Why is the brain uniquely vulnerable to insulin overtreatment in diabetics, even though brain glucose uptake is not insulin-dependent?","choices":["Insulin is required for all brain glucose uptake just like other tissues, so hypoglycemia from overtreatment causes only mild, transient fatigue","The brain stores several hours' worth of glycogen reserves, so brief episodes of hypoglycemia rarely cause any noticeable neurological symptoms","The brain can switch entirely to fat and ketone metabolism within seconds of glucose delivery falling, making it largely resistant to hypoglycemia","The brain relies almost exclusively on glucose for energy, has only about a 2-minute glycogen reserve, and overtreatment causing hypoglycemia can lead to psychotic disturbances and coma"],"answer":3,"exp":"The brain uses glucose for energy almost exclusively and has only about a 2-minute glycogen reserve; because brain glucose delivery uses the GLUT1 transporter (not insulin-dependent GLUT4 like most tissues), overtreatment with insulin causing low blood glucose can rapidly cause psychotic disturbances and coma. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does increased arterial CO2 cause cerebral vasodilation?","choices":["CO2 directly binds specific G-protein coupled receptors on vascular smooth muscle cells, triggering relaxation","CO2 has no direct effect on cerebral blood flow, since only changes in arterial oxygen tension regulate vessel tone","CO2 causes vasoconstriction rather than vasodilation by directly lowering local H+ ion concentration in brain tissue","CO2 diffuses into brain tissue, raising local H+ ion concentration, and H+ ions cause vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased arterial CO2 diffuses into brain tissue, raising the local H+ ion concentration; the resulting rise in H+ causes cerebral vasodilation, increasing cerebral blood flow. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of astrocytes in matching cerebral blood flow to neuronal activity?","choices":["Increased neuronal activity causes glutamate spillover, triggering an astrocytic calcium wave that leads to release of vasodilatory prostaglandins, dilating arterioles","Astrocytes only regulate CSF production and reabsorption at the choroid plexus, playing no role in matching flow to neuronal activity","Astrocytes have no role in blood flow regulation, which is instead governed entirely by direct neuronal innervation of arteriolar smooth muscle","Increased neuronal activity triggers astrocytic release of endothelin, directly constricting nearby arterioles and reducing local blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased neuronal activity leads to increased glutamate spillover at synapses, which triggers an astrocytic calcium wave; this wave leads to release of vasodilatory prostaglandins, causing arterioles to dilate and matching blood flow to neuronal activity. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Over what arterial pressure range is cerebral blood flow (CBF) autoregulated?","choices":["0 to 60 mm Hg","CBF is never autoregulated","60 mm Hg to 150 mm Hg","150 to 300 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cerebral blood flow is autoregulated over a pressure range of approximately 60 to 150 mm Hg; above 160 mm Hg, blood flow increases with pressure and vessels begin to stretch, risking damage and rupture (stroke). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Where is most cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) produced, and approximately how much is formed each day?","choices":["The choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles; about 500 mL/day","The choroid plexus in the fourth ventricle; about 1000 mL/day","The arachnoid granulations lining the dura; about 150 mL/day","The ependymal lining of the third ventricle; about 300 mL/day"],"answer":0,"exp":"CSF is mainly produced by the choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles, with about 500 mL formed each day (compared to a total CSF volume of only about 150 mL), and performs a cushioning function for the brain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How does CSF glucose concentration compare to plasma glucose concentration?","choices":["CSF glucose is identical to plasma glucose","CSF has no glucose at all","CSF glucose is double that of plasma","CSF glucose is about 30% of plasma glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"CSF glucose concentration is about 30% of that of plasma; CSF also has slightly less Na+ than plasma, about 15% higher Cl-, 40% of the K+, and only 1-2% of the plasma protein concentration. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What structural feature makes brain capillaries unique compared to capillaries elsewhere in the body, forming the basis of the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["Brain capillaries have unusually thin endothelial cells with no basement membrane, allowing rapid passive diffusion of most plasma solutes","Brain capillaries are identical in structure to liver sinusoids, with large intercellular gaps that permit free passage of plasma proteins","Brain capillaries are a continuous type with tight junctions between endothelial cells (unique to brain capillaries), reinforced by a thick basement membrane and astrocytic end-feet","Brain capillaries are fenestrated, like those in the kidney glomerulus, allowing free passage of large molecules directly into brain tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by brain endothelial cells with tight junctions between them (a feature unique to brain capillaries), reinforced by a thick basement membrane and astrocytic end-feet, forming a continuous type of capillary that restricts substances to passing through the endothelial cell membrane itself. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What can cross the blood-brain barrier by passive diffusion, and what requires selective transport?","choices":["Nothing crosses the blood-brain barrier without active transport, including water, gases, and lipid-soluble anesthetics","Only large plasma proteins like albumin cross passively, while gases and small lipid molecules require selective transporters","Water, gases (N2, O2, CO2), and small lipid molecules cross by passive diffusion; glucose and amino acids require selective transport","Neurotoxins like botulinum toxin cross freely via specific carrier-mediated transport shared with essential amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"The blood-brain barrier allows water, gases (N2, O2, CO2), and small lipid molecules to cross by passive diffusion, while glucose and amino acids crucial to neural function require selective transport; the barrier prevents entry of potential neurotoxins such as botulinum toxin. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Does removal of the somatic sensory areas of the cortex destroy the ability to perceive pain?","choices":["Removal of the somatosensory cortex has been studied extensively and consistently abolishes both fast and slow pain perception entirely","Yes, pain perception is entirely cortical, since lower brain areas like the thalamus and reticular formation cannot generate conscious sensation","Yes, but only for fast, sharp pain, while slow, aching pain remains fully perceivable through subcortical structures alone","No — pain impulses reaching lower brain areas can still cause conscious perception of pain, suggesting the cortex is more important for determining the quality of pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Removal of the somatic sensory areas of the cortex does not destroy the ability to perceive pain, since pain impulses reaching lower brain areas can still cause conscious perception — the cortex is likely more important for determining the quality/character of pain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is naloxone (Narcan), and how does it work?","choices":["An NSAID that inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, used for chronic inflammatory pain like arthritis and lower back pain","An opioid antagonist that binds opioid receptors and can reverse/block the effects of opioids like heroin, morphine, and fentanyl, used to counter overdose","An opioid agonist that binds opioid receptors and enhances/prolongs the effects of heroin, morphine, and fentanyl, worsening overdose","A synthetic analog of Substance P that binds neurokinin receptors and enhances pain transmission at the dorsal horn"],"answer":1,"exp":"Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist that binds to opioid receptors and reverses/blocks the effects of opioids such as heroin, morphine, oxycodone, and fentanyl, making it effective for countering opiate overdose. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the specific role of the lateral cerebellar hemisphere in motor planning?","choices":["It has no role in motor planning whatsoever, functioning instead exclusively to maintain equilibrium through direct connections to the vestibular nuclei","It communicates with the premotor/sensory cortex and basal ganglia, receiving the movement plan and timing the sequential events to carry it out","It directly generates the plan for movement entirely on its own, with no input required from the premotor cortex, sensory cortex, or basal ganglia","It only processes auditory information, relaying cochlear input to the inferior colliculus rather than participating in any motor timing function"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral cerebellar hemisphere is responsible for the planning and timing of sequential movements — it communicates with the premotor and sensory cortex and the corresponding area of the basal ganglia (where the movement plan originates), receiving the plan and timing the sequential events to carry it out. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What are the four nuclei that make up the basal ganglia?","choices":["Thalamus, hypothalamus, hippocampus, and amygdala -- the four interconnected limbic structures classically grouped together as the basal ganglia","Striatum (caudate and putamen), globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and subthalamus","Frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital association cortex, the four cortical regions that together form the functional basal ganglia","Cerebellum, pons, medulla, and midbrain -- the four brainstem structures traditionally grouped together to form the basal ganglia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basal ganglia consist of four nuclei: the striatum (caudate and putamen), globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and subthalamus — the principal subcortical components of parallel circuits linking the thalamus with the cerebral cortex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What kinds of complex motor activities does the basal ganglia help control?","choices":["Only coordinated movements of the eyes, including saccadic jumps and the smooth pursuit tracking of a moving visual target","Complex patterns of motor activity such as writing, using scissors, throwing balls, shoveling dirt, and aspects of vocalization","Only cardiac and respiratory rhythms, setting the basal pacemaker rate for both the heart and the brainstem's respiratory control centers","Only simple reflexive movements like the patellar or withdrawal reflex, with no involvement in any voluntary or learned motor sequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basal ganglia control complex patterns of motor activity, including writing, using scissors, throwing balls, shoveling dirt, and some aspects of vocalization — thought to function in the timing, scaling, and initiation of motion. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of the caudate circuit, given its extensive input from cortical association areas?","choices":["A role in the cognitive control of motor functions, since most motor actions occur as a result of a sequence of thoughts","It has no connections to the rest of the basal ganglia at all, receiving cortical input directly and projecting straight back to that same cortical area","Direct control of reflexive spinal withdrawal only, bypassing the cortex entirely to synapse directly onto spinal cord interneurons","Exclusively controls eye movements, receiving input only from the visual cortex rather than from the broader cortical association areas"],"answer":0,"exp":"The caudate circuit receives large input from association areas of the cortex and projects mostly to the globus pallidus; because most motor actions arise from a sequence of thoughts, the caudate circuit is thought to play a role in the cognitive control of motor functions. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of the putamen circuit?","choices":["A role in the execution of motor movement, via connections mostly from premotor and supplemental motor cortex, through the putamen, and back to the motor cortex","It solely mediates emotional processing, receiving input exclusively from the amygdala and cingulate cortex rather than from any motor cortical area","It plays no role in motor function at all, functioning instead purely as a relay station for somatosensory information heading to the thalamus","It is responsible for producing speech only, connecting exclusively to Broca's area rather than to the premotor or supplemental motor cortex"],"answer":0,"exp":"The putamen circuit runs mostly from the premotor and supplemental motor cortex to the putamen and back to the motor cortex, and is thought to play a role in the execution of motor movement (as opposed to the caudate circuit's more cognitive role). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the primary role of the cortical level in the integration of motor control, according to this lecture?","choices":["Maintaining equilibrium by adjusting axial and limb muscle tone alone, a role handled by the brainstem level rather than any cortical involvement","Directly contracting individual muscle fibers with no intermediary circuitry, bypassing the spinal cord's preprogrammed pattern generators entirely","Issuing commands that set into motion patterns already available in the spinal cord, controlling their intensity and modifying timing","Preprogramming basic patterns of movement like withdrawal reflexes, a function that instead belongs entirely to the spinal cord level of integration"],"answer":2,"exp":"At the cortical level of motor integration, the cortex issues commands that set into motion patterns of movement already available in the spinal cord, controlling the intensity of the movement and modifying its timing — distinct from the spinal cord level (preprogrammed patterns) and brainstem level (equilibrium via axial tone). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"According to the overall scheme of motor integration, what dual role do the basal ganglia play?","choices":["They assist the cortex in executing subconscious but learned patterns of movement, and help plan sequential patterns to accomplish a purposeful task","They only inhibit all movement globally, acting purely as a brake mechanism with no independent role in planning purposeful sequences of motion","They have no functional role in motor integration at all, serving instead purely as a relay for olfactory and limbic signals to the cortex","They function only at the spinal cord level, integrating reflex circuitry rather than assisting the cortex in learned, subconscious movement patterns"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the overall integration of motor function, the basal ganglia assist the cortex in executing subconscious but learned patterns of movement, and also help plan sequential patterns of movement needed to accomplish a purposeful task. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"From which thalamic nuclei does the motor cortex receive input important for coordinating function between the motor cortex, basal ganglia, and cerebellum?","choices":["The ventrolateral and ventroanterior nuclei of the thalamus","The ventrobasal complex of the thalamus only","The pulvinar nucleus of the thalamus","The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventrolateral and ventroanterior nuclei of the thalamus provide input to the motor cortex important for coordinating function between the motor cortex, basal ganglia, and cerebellum, while the intralaminar nuclei help control the level of excitability of the motor cortex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Approximately how many giant pyramidal (Betz) cells must be excited simultaneously to cause muscle contraction?","choices":["10,000","1-2","All 34,000 Betz cells at once","50-100"],"answer":3,"exp":"Excitation of approximately 50-100 giant pyramidal (Betz) cells is needed to cause muscle contraction; most corticospinal fibers synapse with interneurons, though some synapse directly with alpha motor neurons, especially those innervating the fingers and hand in the cervical enlargement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How is the brainstem functionally related to the spinal cord?","choices":["It exclusively controls voluntary fine motor movement of the hands and fingers, a function that is actually performed by the primary motor cortex rather than the brainstem","It only processes visual information relayed from the retina, forwarding it to the occipital cortex with no involvement in motor or sensory function for the head","It acts as an extension of the spinal cord, performing similar motor and sensory functions for the face and head via the cranial nerves, and contains centers for stereotypic movement and equilibrium","It has no functional relationship to the spinal cord at all, operating instead as an entirely separate and independently controlled neural structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brainstem functions as an extension of the spinal cord, performing motor and sensory functions for the face and head (via the cranial nerves) similar to how the spinal cord functions for the body below the head, and it contains centers for stereotypic movement and equilibrium. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What role does sensory feedback (from muscle spindles, tactile receptors, and proprioceptors) play in motor control?","choices":["It plays no role once a movement has begun, since the initial motor cortex command is thought to fully specify the entire trajectory in advance","It fine-tunes muscle movement, allowing auto-correction for length mismatch in the spindle and informing the motor cortex of the effectiveness of an intended action","It only matters for spinal reflexes, never contributing meaningfully to voluntary movement once the motor cortex has issued its initial command","It is only relevant to cerebellar function, playing no role at all in informing the motor cortex about the effectiveness of an intended action"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sensory feedback from muscle spindles, tactile receptors, and proprioceptors fine-tunes muscle movement — a length mismatch detected by the spindle causes auto-correction, and skin compression provides feedback to the motor cortex on how effective an intended action has been. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Besides the direct corticospinal pathway, what indirect pathways carry signals from the motor cortex?","choices":["Signals to the basal ganglia, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei","Signals only to the auditory cortex and inferior colliculus","Signals only to the retina and visual cortex","Only one output pathway exists -- the corticospinal tract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Besides the direct pathway (corticospinal tract, for discrete detailed movement), the motor cortex sends signals via indirect pathways to the basal ganglia, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the amygdala's key role within the limbic system?","choices":["Fear, threat detection, and emotional learning","Permanent long-term memory storage and retrieval","Fine motor coordination of speech muscles","Autonomic regulation of body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"The amygdala is key for fear, threat detection, and emotional learning; it receives input from other limbic areas and most of the cortex, and sends output back to the cortex as well as into the hippocampus, septum, and hypothalamus. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which of the following is a \"vegetative\" function controlled by the hypothalamus, as the major output pathway of the limbic system?","choices":["Voluntary control of motor speech production, coordinating respiratory timing and articulation muscles together with Broca's area and the cerebellum","Neurogenic control of arterial pressure, body temperature regulation, fluid volume regulation, and endocrine gland secretion","Formation of long-term declarative memory, requiring rehearsal and consolidation over hours to days via hippocampal and thalamic circuits working together","Voluntary fine motor control of the hands, mediated through the primary motor cortex and corticospinal tract rather than any hypothalamic pathway"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hypothalamus, the major output pathway of the limbic system, controls vegetative functions including neurogenic control of arterial pressure, regulation of body temperature, regulation of fluid volume, and regulation of endocrine gland secretion (growth hormone, thyroid hormone, glucocorticoids, sex hormones). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic region is associated with eating, thirst, general activity level, and rage?","choices":["Periventricular nucleus","Lateral hypothalamus","Anterior hypothalamus","Ventromedial nucleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral hypothalamus is associated with eating, thirst, general level of activity, and rage; the ventromedial nucleus is associated with satiety and tranquility, and the periventricular nucleus with fear and punishment reactions. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where is the reward center located in the brain, and which type of response (reward or punishment) takes precedence?","choices":["Reward center around the medial forebrain bundle (lateral and ventromedial hypothalamus); punishment always takes precedence over reward","Reward center around the septal nuclei and anterior cingulate cortex; reward always takes precedence over punishment","Reward and punishment centers overlap entirely within the amygdala, so neither system takes functional precedence","Punishment center in the central gray around the aqueduct extending into periventricular zones; reward always takes precedence over punishment"],"answer":0,"exp":"The reward center is located around the medial forebrain bundle (lateral and ventromedial hypothalamus), while the punishment center is located in the central gray around the aqueduct extending into periventricular zones; punishment always takes precedence over reward. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the evolutionary origin of the hippocampus, and how does this relate to its function?","choices":["It originated as part of the olfactory cortex, historically involved in determining the importance of incoming information (e.g., is it food, danger, or a mate) via smell","It originated as part of the visual cortex; in lower animals, vision was the dominant sense for detecting predators, and the early hippocampus helped discriminate threatening shapes and movement","It has no evolutionary connection to sensory processing at all, instead arising as a late elaboration of the neocortex devoted to abstract symbolic reasoning","It originated as part of the primitive motor cortex; in lower animals, stereotyped motor sequences were critical for survival, and the early hippocampus helped select which movement pattern to execute"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hippocampus originated as part of the olfactory cortex; in lower animals, smell was an important determinant of behavior (is it good to eat, does it smell dangerous, is it sexually inviting), and the early hippocampus was involved in determining the importance of incoming sensory information. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How do reward and punishment influence whether the hippocampus stores information as memory?","choices":["Only punishment-associated experiences are consolidated into memory; pleasurable experiences are processed instead by the amygdala and are never stored as long-term memory by the hippocampus","Reward and punishment have no bearing on hippocampal storage -- the hippocampus stores every sensory experience equally regardless of its emotional significance","A person becomes habituated to indifferent stimuli, but learns (stores as memory) sensory experiences that cause pain or pleasure — the hippocampus drives rehearsal and consolidation of these experiences","Only reward-associated experiences drive hippocampal consolidation; painful experiences are instead routed exclusively through the spinal withdrawal reflex circuitry"],"answer":2,"exp":"A person becomes habituated to indifferent stimuli but learns any sensory experience that causes pain or pleasure; the hippocampus provides the drive to rehearse and consolidate these emotionally significant sensory experiences into memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the functional role of the cingulate gyrus within the limbic system?","choices":["Exclusively regulating pituitary hormone secretion","Integrating emotion with cognition and attention","Detecting angular acceleration of head rotation","Generating the motor pattern for speech sounds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cingulate gyrus integrates emotion with cognition and attention, serving as a key limbic structure linking emotional processing to higher cognitive function. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the functional significance of the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus?","choices":["It serves as the primary site of long-term declarative memory storage","It functions as the body's \"master clock,\" regulating circadian rhythms","It controls only the consensual pupillary light reflex","It functions solely to relay retinal input to the visual cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus functions as the master clock of the body, coordinating circadian rhythms. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Does destruction of the cerebral cortex prevent an individual from thinking, according to this lecture?","choices":["Thinking occurs entirely within the cerebellum, since the cortex functions solely as a relay station for raw sensory data and has no role in generating conscious thought","Yes -- thinking is impossible without an intact cortex, since the cortex is the sole structure capable of generating any pattern of neural activity","The neural mechanism of thought is fully understood and is localized entirely to the prefrontal cortex, with no other brain region contributing","No — destruction of the cerebral cortex does not prevent thinking, though depth of thought and level of awareness may be reduced"],"answer":3,"exp":"Destruction of the cerebral cortex does not prevent an individual from thinking, although the depth of thought and level of awareness may be reduced; the neural mechanism underlying thought itself is not fully known, but is thought to involve a specific pattern of simultaneous neural activity across many brain areas. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is a \"memory trace\"?","choices":["A type of neurotransmitter released specifically during rehearsal that binds calcium channels to strengthen synapses","A physical scar of degenerated tissue left behind permanently at the site of a formerly active synapse","A specialized structure found only within Purkinje cells of the cerebellum that stores motor sequences","A specific pattern or pathway of signal transmission that, once established, can be reactivated by the thinking mind to reproduce the memory"],"answer":3,"exp":"A memory trace is a specific pattern or pathway of signal transmission resulting from a change in synaptic transmission capability; once established, it can be activated by the thinking mind to reproduce the associated memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"In the pathway for visual communication (reading a word aloud), what happens after visual input is processed in the parietal-temporal-occipital association cortex (angular gyrus)?","choices":["The signal is discarded at the angular gyrus, since visual word input cannot be converted to a phonetic representation without first passing through the primary auditory cortex","It is relayed to the hippocampus for permanent storage as a visual memory trace, without ever being converted into a motor speech pattern or reaching Broca's area","It bypasses Wernicke's area entirely, passing straight from the angular gyrus to Broca's area and then to the motor cortex for speech muscles","Visual input reaches full interpretation in Wernicke's area, then passes to Broca's area for motor formation of the word before signals go to the motor cortex for speech muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"For visual communication, after the primary visual area receives input and it is processed in the angular gyrus region, the visual input reaches full interpretation in Wernicke's area, then passes to Broca's area for motor formation of the word, and finally to the motor cortex to control speech muscles. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What effect does stimulation of a \"facilitator terminal\" have on habituation?","choices":["It prevents habituation from occurring","It converts habituation into long-term memory instantly","It accelerates habituation","It has no effect on habituation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Stimulation of a facilitator terminal prevents habituation, counteracting the progressive decline in calcium channel activity and neurotransmitter release that normally underlies habituation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
